course: dictionary
lesson: 9420
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Squint» is one of them. As a verb, to squint means to look with your eyes partly closed. As a noun, a squint is a condition where the eyes look in different directions.
explain: «A boy in Halifax squints at the board and sits closer and closer.» The words around squints give the clue: he is trying to see the board, so he partly closes his eyes to see it better. His teacher notices, and an eye test shows he needs glasses.
explain: «Walk out of a dark cinema into bright sunshine and you squint.» The clue is bright sunshine. When a word has two meanings, pick the one that fits the sentence, then swap it in to check that it makes sense.
explain: Phrases help too, such as squint at and squint in the sun. Squinting briefly does not damage your eyes, but needing to squint is a sign to get an eye test.
fact: «Squint» can be a verb (to squint) or a noun (a squint).
fact: To squint means to look with your eyes partly closed, for example in bright sun or to see something better.
fact: A squint is a condition where the eyes look in different directions; doctors call it strabismus.
fact: Squinting briefly does not damage your eyes, but needing to squint is a sign to get an eye test.
fact: Orthoptists are eye workers who help with squints and lazy eyes.
exercise: «Walk out of a dark cinema into bright sunshine and you squint.» What does squint mean here? | To run very fast | A condition where the eyes look in different directions | To shut your eyes tightly and cry | *To look with your eyes partly closed
exercise: «A squint, or strabismus, is when the eyes don't line up.» What is a squint in this sentence? | A test at the school | A pair of new glasses | Eyes wandering in the dark | *A condition where the eyes look in different directions
exercise: Which phrase uses squint to mean looking with your eyes partly closed in bright light? | *squint in the sun | a squint in the mirror | squint at the stars at night | a squint, or strabismus
test: The boy in Halifax squints at the board. Why is he squinting? | *He cannot see the board clearly | He is fast asleep | His teacher told him to play a game | He is laughing at a joke
test: Which word is a verb in «He squints at the sun»? | sun | *squints | He | at
exam: Which meaning fits «it made him squint» from the story? | *Made him look with his eyes partly closed | Made him fall asleep | Made him laugh loudly | Made him shut his eyes for ever
